Director - felt that environmental statement represented a comprehensive statement setting forth criteria for protection of natural resources on Federal lands. Suggested that statement include definitions of ORV, public lands, etc., and define status of ORV use on public lands which are open to mineral entry and established mining claims. Felt that much of the description of economic impacts in the statement is inappropriate and unnecessary. Fointed out that the "open or closed" policies of each of the bureaus should be consistent. Stated belief that completed studies on noise pollution contain enough information for definite acceptable noise levels to be spelled out, in decibels at measured distances, for different types ORV and outdoor public use areas;
#106 National Park Service, Assistant Director, Cooperative Services - suggested that section on page 5 of environmental statement describing operating criteria for ORV use on Interior lands be made as clear as possible concerning "reckless and careless manner" and referred us to 36 CFR 4.14. Suggested that evidence be submitted to support claim that ORV can strengthen family ties. Expressed concern about lack of attention in environmental statement to protection of historic, archeological, and paleontological resources. Indicated belief that Section 2(b) of Executive Order 11593, dated 5/13/71, must be observed in contemplating use of ORV on federally-owned lands, and that statement should indicate whether compliance with E.O. 11593 will be assured during implementation of E.O. 11644. Also, indicated belief that statement should indicate whether compliance with Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act. (P.L. 89-665) will be assured during implementation of E.O. 11644;
# 9 (e) Geological Survey, Acting Director  advised that they have no comments on subject ORV environmental statement.
# 68 (f) Bureau of Indian Affairs, Director, Office of Planning  advised that subject statement had been reviewed and indicated their belief that it adequately covers environmental considerations;
General Comments
The bulk of the comments received concerning the draft ORV environmental statement expressed concurrence with the statement and the proposed actions for implementing Executive Order 11644 on Interior lands.
In general, these comments centered around the following subjects relating to the various aspects of off-road vehicle use:
1. Resource use recognition (mining)
2. Open-until-closed versus closed-until-open policy regarding use of Federal lands for ORV.
